   "id": "55132cc6",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Array computations with NumPy\n",
    "\n",
    "In the previous chapter we summarized data using plain Python lists, the `statistics` module, and considerable manual work. That approach worked, but it was tedious: to do almost anything to *every* value in a list (add a tax to every price, convert every value to different units, or just add two lists of numbers together), we had to handle the values one at a time.\n",
    "\n",
    "In this chapter we introduce **NumPy** (short for \"Numerical Python\"), the standard Python library for working with numerical data. NumPy gives us a new kind of container called an **array**, which makes numerical computations shorter to write and faster to run. Arrays are the foundation that nearly every other data science tool in Python, including the Pandas package we'll use later, is built on top of.\n",
    "\n",
    "By the end of this chapter you'll be able to load numerical data into arrays, transform and summarize that data in a single step, filter it down to the values you care about, and even treat images as arrays of numbers.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Why arrays instead of lists?\n",
    "\n",
    "To see why lists alone aren't quite enough, suppose we have the price of a gallon of gas (in dollars) for three weeks, stored in a list:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 1,
   "id": "bea21cce",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "weekly_prices = [3.05, 3.04, 3.11]"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "a58e953e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Now imagine the government adds a \\$2-per-gallon tax, and we want the new price for each week. It would be natural to *try* multiplying or adding to the list, but lists don't behave the way we might hope:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 2,
   "id": "a947e725",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "[3.05, 3.04, 3.11, 3.05, 3.04, 3.11]"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 2,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# Multiplying a list by 2 repeats the list instead of doubling each value\n",
    "weekly_prices * 2"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "bd981f4d",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "As you can see, that doesn't double each price: multiplying a list by a number just *repeats* the list. And adding a number to a list doesn't work at all; it causes an error. To actually add \\$2 to each price, we'd have to reach into the list and update each value individually:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 3,
   "id": "b99933b6",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "[5.05, 5.04, 5.109999999999999]"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 3,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# Adding $2 to each price, one value at a time, is tedious\n",
    "[weekly_prices[0] + 2, weekly_prices[1] + 2, weekly_prices[2] + 2]"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "91b0e2ea",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "This works for three prices, but not for a whole year of weekly data.\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-note}\n",
    "Python does have a tool for repeating an action many times without writing it out by hand: the **loop**, which we cover later in @sec-iteration-functions. But for the whole-dataset calculations that are common in data science, the array approach shown in this chapter is usually shorter, faster, and easier to read, so we will favor it throughout the book.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "NumPy lets us express the same computation in one line. First, import the library (by near-universal convention, NumPy is imported under the short name `np`):"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 4,
   "id": "5ce1f37a",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import numpy as np"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "87c5c52b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Now we can turn our list into a NumPy array with `np.array()` and simply add 2:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 5,
   "id": "16beb237",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "array([5.05, 5.04, 5.11])"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 5,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "price_array = np.array([3.05, 3.04, 3.11])\n",
    "\n",
    "# Add the $2 tax to every price at once\n",
    "price_array + 2"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "9aa963db",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The operation was applied to every element automatically. This idea, applying an operation to every element of an array at once, is called **vectorization**, and it is the central idea behind NumPy's usefulness. Compared to lists, arrays give us two main advantages:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Ease of expression.** Element-wise arithmetic like `price_array + 2` lets us say what we mean in a single short expression.\n",
    "2. **Speed.** NumPy performs these operations in fast, pre-compiled code and stores values more compactly than a list, so computations on large arrays run faster and use less memory.\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-tip title=\"Exercise\" #exercise-array_computations-vectorize}\n",
    "\n",
    "Here are three more weekly gas prices stored in a list: `[3.20, 3.15, 3.18]`. Turn this list into a NumPy array, then use a single expression (no loop) to apply a 10% discount to every price.\n",
    "\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-note title=\"Solution\" #solution-array_computations-vectorize collapse=\"true\"}"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 6,
   "id": "53b1dbea",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "array([2.88 , 2.835, 2.862])"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 6,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "more_prices = np.array([3.20, 3.15, 3.18])\n",
    "\n",
    "# Paying 90% of each price applies a 10% discount to every element at once\n",
    "more_prices * 0.9"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "cb06787f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## An example dataset: a year of U.S. gas prices\n",
    "\n",
    "To explore NumPy we'll look at the price of gasoline in the United States over the course of a year. Gas prices change every week, so the data gives us a natural sequence to explore: how prices moved, how much they varied, and what fraction of weeks fell below a given threshold.\n",
    "\n",
    "Our data comes from **FRED** (Federal Reserve Economic Data), a large public database of economic time series maintained by the Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis. We'll use the series for the U.S. average weekly retail price of regular gasoline, which has the code `GASREGW`. The hidden cell below uses the `pandas-datareader` package to download that series, falling back to a saved copy of the data if FRED cannot be reached. What matters is that running it gives us two NumPy arrays: `gas_prices`, holding the price for each week, and `gas_dates`, holding the corresponding date for each price."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 7,
   "id": "2835dcad",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "#| echo: false\n",
    "\n",
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "\n",
    "# Try to download the data live from FRED; if that fails (for example, when there\n",
    "# is no internet connection), fall back to a saved snapshot of the same year.\n",
    "try:\n",
    "    from pandas_datareader.fred import FredReader\n",
    "    gas_data_all = FredReader(\"GASREGW\", start=\"2024-06-01\", end=\"2026-01-15\").read().reset_index()\n",
    "    gas_year = gas_data_all[(gas_data_all[\"DATE\"] >= \"2025-01-01\") & (gas_data_all[\"DATE\"] < \"2026-01-01\")]\n",
    "except Exception:\n",
    "    gas_year = pd.read_csv(\"data/gas_prices_GASREGW_2025.csv\", parse_dates=[\"DATE\"])\n",
    "\n",
    "# Pull the prices and dates out of the data table as NumPy arrays\n",
    "gas_prices = gas_year[\"GASREGW\"].values\n",
    "gas_dates = gas_year[\"DATE\"].values"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "1df3662c",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Take a look at the prices we just loaded:"
   ]
  },
  {
