    "\n",
    "## Higher-dimensional arrays and image processing\n",
    "\n",
    "Every array we've used so far has been one-dimensional: a single row of numbers. But arrays can have more than one dimension. A two-dimensional array is just a grid of numbers, with rows and columns like a spreadsheet.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Creating and indexing matrices\n",
    "\n",
    "A **matrix** is simply a 2D array, and the most direct way to build one is to pass `np.array()` a list of lists, with each inner list becoming one row:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 48,
   "id": "67dd44b9",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "array([[1, 2, 3],\n",
       "       [4, 5, 6]])"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 48,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "matrix = np.array([\n",
    "    [1, 2, 3],\n",
    "    [4, 5, 6],\n",
    "])\n",
    "\n",
    "matrix"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "3aa55b50",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The `.shape` confirms the structure: 2 rows and 3 columns."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 49,
   "id": "850b9422",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "(2, 3)"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 49,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "matrix.shape"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "2204ef1e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Indexing a 2D array takes two positions separated by a comma: a row, then a column. `matrix[0, 1]` is the value in row 0, column 1:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 50,
   "id": "c5a6b4b2",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "np.int64(2)"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 50,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "matrix[0, 1]"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "cd73edaa",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "A `:` in either position means \"every value along that axis.\" `matrix[1, :]` pulls out all of row 1, and `matrix[:, 2]` pulls out all of column 2:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 51,
   "id": "ed3c0276",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "[array([4, 5, 6]), array([3, 6])]"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 51,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "[matrix[1, :], matrix[:, 2]]"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "45ba36ef",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Slicing combines with indexing the same way: `matrix[0:2, 1:3]` keeps rows 0 and 1, and within those rows, columns 1 and 2:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 52,
   "id": "ebc77ac4",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "array([[2, 3],\n",
       "       [5, 6]])"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 52,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "matrix[0:2, 1:3]"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "57500c57",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The `axis` argument in NumPy functions specifies which dimension to operate along. On a 2D array, `axis=0` operates *down the rows*, producing one result per column, and `axis=1` operates *across the columns*, producing one result per row:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 53,
   "id": "b0e427eb",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "array([5, 7, 9])"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 53,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# Sum down the rows (axis=0): one total per column\n",
    "np.sum(matrix, axis=0)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 54,
   "id": "9ffcffd5",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "array([ 6, 15])"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 54,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# Sum across the columns (axis=1): one total per row\n",
    "np.sum(matrix, axis=1)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "08d12363",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We will use this same idea with `axis=2` when working with 3D color images later in this section.\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-tip title=\"Exercise\" #exercise-array_computations-matrix}\n",
    "\n",
    "Create the matrix `[[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6], [7, 8, 9]]` using `np.array()`. Then use indexing to pull out the value 7, and slicing to pull out the entire bottom row.\n",
    "\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-note title=\"Solution\" #solution-array_computations-matrix collapse=\"true\"}"
