   "source": [
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "### Grayscale images are 2D arrays\n",
    "\n",
    "We can create a 2D array directly. Here we start with a 100×100 grid of zeros using `np.zeros()`, then use slicing to set a square block of values in the middle to 1. Note that the `[100, 100]` we pass to `np.zeros()` is not a list of *values* (the way `np.array([3.05, 3.04])` was). It describes the *shape* we want: 100 rows and 100 columns. NumPy functions that accept a shape always expect it as a single list or tuple, not as separate arguments."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 56,
   "id": "803ddc69",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# A 100x100 grid of zeros (100 rows, 100 columns)\n",
    "grid = np.zeros([100, 100])\n",
    "\n",
    "# Set a square block of the grid to 1 using slicing on both dimensions\n",
    "grid[30:70, 30:70] = 1"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d7451e43",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Why make a grid of numbers? Because **a grayscale image is exactly that**: a 2D array where each number is the brightness of a pixel. If we display our grid as an image, the 1s show up as a white square on a black background:"
   ]
